    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grok 4.7";
    dc:identifier "grok-4.7-high-fast".
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-epilogue-02--grok> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grok 4.7 on thompson1917-epilogue-02: not-empirically-resolvable, resolvability 0.12";
    dc:description "The epilogue asserts a general coincidence of mathematical beauty and usefulness, with the bee's cell as an illustration. Usefulness can be defined for particular structures. The universal identification of the most beautiful with the most useful has no measurable contrast that would refute it.";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Proposed test with existing data: The claim identifies whatever is most beautiful and regular in natural form with whatever is most useful. Beauty and regularity are not fixed measured variables, so no dataset can show that the most regular forms are the most useful across nature generally.";
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/evidenceAgainst> "A special case such as the honeycomb can be studied mechanically without giving the universal wording a decision criterion.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasConfidence> "0.74"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasScore> "0.124"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasVerdict> "not-empirically-resolvable".
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-epilogue-02--grok--analysis-feasibility> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
